helpbuoy-relay for Debian
====================

The relay runs as the systemd service helpbuoy-relay, as the system user
helpbuoy-relay, with its host key, agents and registered clients in
/var/lib/helpbuoy-relay. It listens on :5959 by default; change that in
/etc/default/helpbuoy-relay and run "systemctl restart helpbuoy-relay".

Every agent and client connects to that one port, so open it in the
firewall (and forward it on the router if the relay is behind NAT). The
apps use port 5959 when no port is given, so with the default users only
type the host name, e.g. relay.example.com.

Admin commands go through helpbuoy-relayctl, which runs helpbuoy-relay as the
service user on the service's state:

  sudo helpbuoy-relayctl hostkey              # fingerprint to give to agents
  sudo helpbuoy-relayctl agent add alice 'ssh-ed25519 AAAA...'
  sudo helpbuoy-relayctl agent list
  sudo helpbuoy-relayctl agent remove alice
  sudo helpbuoy-relayctl client list
  sudo helpbuoy-relayctl client remove SHA256:...
  sudo helpbuoy-relayctl title 'Example Inc'
  sudo helpbuoy-relayctl instruction 'Call +1 234 567 890 for support'

The running relay picks up changes within a second. Logs are in the
journal (journalctl -u helpbuoy-relay) and in /var/lib/helpbuoy-relay/relay.log.

"apt purge helpbuoy-relay" deletes /var/lib/helpbuoy-relay, including the host key:
clients registered with this relay would then see a changed relay identity.
